    "explanation": "Rider and Driver, the claim is decided on the recorded route and distances. The recommended route was New Upper Changi Road, Bedok Road, Upper East Coast Road and Marine Parade Road, 4.6 km. The route actually driven was New Upper Changi Road, Jalan Bilal, Siglap Road, Upper East Coast Road and Marine Parade Road, 5.2 km. The trip record and GPS fixes support the rider's account that the vehicle left Bedok Road at Jalan Bilal and rejoined at Siglap Road. Under POL-RD-22 the deviation is the recorded actual distance minus the recorded optimal distance: 5.2 km minus 4.6 km equals 0.60 km. POL-RD-22 sets a 1.0 km tolerance, so this deviation is within tolerance. The GPS trace's 4.315 km figure is not used to re-measure distance under POL-RD-22; it shows the route taken only. The LTA traffic advisory for Bedok Road during the trip window reports type NONE, no incident, roadwork or closure, and speed bands 4-6, so POL-RD-23 does not justify the reroute. The chat log is complete and contains no in-trip notice or acknowledgment of the deviation, and there is no call recording, so POL-RD-21's notice requirement was not met. Even so, because the deviation is 1.0 km or less, POL-RD-24(e) applies: a not-traffic-justified deviation within tolerance gives NO_ACTION. The excess distance fare on POL-RD-24's formula would be 0.60 km x 0.72 SGD per km, which is 0.43 SGD, not the 2.00 SGD claimed, and it is not payable within tolerance. The 9.42 SGD metered fare stands. NO_ACTION is party-relative and leaves the present state in place, with no refund to the rider and no driver compensation. The disputed value is 2.00 SGD and neither profile has a fraud flag; POL-RD-25 requires 0.80 confidence for a route deviation and the confidence here is 0.95 on complete weight-1.0 artifacts. No clause produces a referral."
